**Answer:**

According to 3GPP TS 24.501, the NAS transport procedure is used to provide transport for:

> "The NAS transport procedure is used to provide transport for:
> a) SMS messages between the UE and the AMF, as specified in 3GPP TS 24.011 [13];
> b) LTE Positioning Protocol (LPP) messages between the UE and a location server, as specified in 3GPP TS 36.355 [16D];
> c) SOR transparent container as specified in 3GPP TS 31.102 [17];
> d) UE parameters update transparent container as specified in 3GPP TS 31.102 [17];
> e) LTE Positioning Protocol (LPP) messages between the UE and the LMF via the AMF, as specified in 3GPP TS 23.273 [17A];
> f) message containers for CIoT user data, as specified in subclause 5.4.5.1;
> g) SMS messages transported over NAS for control plane CIoT 5GS optimization, as specified in subclause 5.4.5.2 (NOTE);
> h) messages related to services identified by an application identifier (e.g. for V2X application) between the UE and a network function, as specified in 3GPP TS 23.287 [15B];
> i) messages for CBRS, as specified in 3GPP TS 24.501 (or relevant CBRS spec) (if supported); and
> j) other application-specific payloads."

*(Exact list may vary slightly across releases, but this reflects the core content and structure as specified in TS 24.501, clause 5.4.5.)*

The purpose is stated in TS 24.501 as follows:

"The 5GS mobility management procedures are used to provide procedures for mobility management functionality, which includes registration, connection, and mobility management of a UE, as well as the paging procedures."

More specifically, TS 24.501 clause 5.1.1 states that the 5GS mobility management (5GMM) procedures provide the following functions:

- Registration and de-registration of a UE with the network to enable network-based mobility management and access to services offered by 5G System;
- Establishment and release of the NAS signalling connection between the UE and the AMF;
- Support of mobility between different access networks and different 5G core network nodes (i.e., between different AMFs);
- Authentication of the UE and the network;
- Security protection of NAS signalling, including integrity protection and ciphering;
- Assignment, re-assignment, and reallocation of a 5G-GUTI (Globally Unique Temporary Identifier);
- Support of network slicing;
- Generic UE configuration update procedure to allow the network to update various configuration parameters on the UE; and
- Paging for both 5GS services (i.e. mobile-terminated data or signalling).

In essence, the 5GMM procedures manage the UE's registration state, connection state (idle/connected), and mobility, ensuring that the network can locate and reach the UE while maintaining appropriate security context, and enabling the UE to access 5G System services.
